---
// Install: the paths that exist, the sandbox each platform needs, upgrade, and
// the first command. Every command here was run with --help on the release
// this page is built from; the two transcripts are captured (see `captured`).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Table from '../components/Table.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { readRepoFile, siteFacts } from '../lib/repo.mjs';
import { GH_URL, ISSUES_URL, PYPI_URL } from '../lib/site-nav.mjs';

const { version, contract } = siteFacts();

// The Python floor is the package's own, never typed here.
const floor = /^requires-python\s*=\s*">=\s*([\d.]+)"/m.exec(readRepoFile('pyproject.toml'))?.[1];
if (!floor) throw new Error('pyproject.toml has no requires-python floor');

// Captured: `skep --version`. The two numbers are the build-time values.
const versionOut = `
$ skep --version
skep ${version} (worker contract ${contract})
`;

// Captured: `skep doctor` on a home that has never been onboarded.
const doctor = `
$ skep doctor
Skep: blocked

Required checks:
- profile: blocked - Personal profile is missing. Next step: run setup --personal

Personal surface:
- provider: unconfigured model=unconfigured
- coding_worker: no worker provider configured
- engine aider: MISSING — 'aider' not on PATH (sandbox-confined; needs verify_command)
- engine builtin: ok — …/bin/python (skep's own worker)
- engine claude_code: NO AUTH ENV — set one of CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — …/bin/claude (sandbox-confined; needs verify_command)
- engine codex: NO AUTH ENV — set one of OPENAI_API_KEY — …/bin/codex (sandbox-confined; needs verify_command)
  ChatGPT login: none (no Codex login on this machine (\`codex login\`))
- engine pi: MISSING — 'pi' not on PATH (sandbox-confined; needs verify_command)
- provider key relay: off — builtin workers hold the provider key (set provider_key_relay to relay it); claude_code, codex, aider and pi authenticate from their own auth env (not relayed)
- approvals: 0 pending
- memory: blocked at ~/.skep/supervisor/supervisor.sqlite3

Runtime checks:
- sandbox: ready - bubblewrap backend is usable. Landlock ABI 10: a worker connects only to unix sockets in its own roots or ones it made itself.
`;

const pipx = `
# Debian, Ubuntu (Fedora: sudo dnf install pipx)
$ sudo apt install pipx
# puts ~/.local/bin on PATH; open a new shell afterwards
$ pipx ensurepath
$ pipx install skep
$ skep --version
`;
const uv = `
# installed, on PATH
$ uv tool install skep
# or run one command without installing
$ uvx skep --version
`;
const pip = `
$ python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
$ pip install skep
`;
const source = `
$ git clone ${GH_URL}.git
$ cd skep
$ uv sync --frozen
$ uv run skep --version
`;
const bwrap = `
# Debian, Ubuntu
$ sudo apt install bubblewrap
# Fedora
$ sudo dnf install bubblewrap
# the last line reports the sandbox
$ skep doctor
`;
const compose = `
$ git clone ${GH_URL}.git && cd skep
$ docker compose up -d
# the access token is printed in the log
$ docker compose logs skep
`;
---

<Base
  title="Install"
  description="Install skep from PyPI with pipx, uv or pip, or from source. The sandbox each platform needs, how to upgrade, and the first command to run."
>
  <Section eyebrow="Install" lead={`skep is a Python package for macOS and Linux. It needs Python ${floor} or newer and Git. Install it, check the sandbox, then let the guided first run do the rest.`}>
    <h1 slot="heading" class="t-h2">Install skep</h1>
    <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items: start">
      <div class="grid">
        <CodeBlock code="$ pipx install skep && skep onboard" title="Install and first run" />
        <p class="t-small c-mut">
          <a class="link-arrow" href="/first-run.html">What skep onboard does</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Terminal code={versionOut} title="check the install" captured="skep --version; the two numbers are filled in from this release's sources" />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="paths" band eyebrow="Install paths" title="Four ways to get the package" lead="All four install the same package from PyPI or from the repository. Pick one.">
    <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items: start">
      <div class="grid">
        <h3 class="t-h3">pipx (recommended)</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            An isolated app with <code>skep</code> on your PATH. On Debian and Ubuntu the system
            Python refuses a bare <code>pip install</code> (PEP 668); pipx is the way around it.
            On macOS, install pipx as <a href="https://pipx.pypa.io/stable/installation/">its own guide</a> says.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock code={pipx} title="pipx" />
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <h3 class="t-h3">uv</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <code>uv tool install</code> does the same job as pipx. <code>uvx</code> runs one
            command from a temporary environment; it needs a subcommand, because
            <code>skep</code> alone only prints its usage.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock code={uv} title="uv" />
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <h3 class="t-h3">pip, inside a virtual environment</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Fine when you manage the environment yourself. <code>skep</code> is on PATH only while that environment is active.</p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock code={pip} title="pip" />
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <h3 class="t-h3">From source</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            What contributors use. It needs <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/">uv</a>,
            and every command becomes <code>uv run skep …</code>.
            <code>bash scripts/install.sh --dry-run</code> prints what the installer script would do
            (sync, the bubblewrap reminder on Linux, the first commands) without doing it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock code={source} title="source checkout" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="sandbox" eyebrow="Sandbox prerequisite" title="What each platform needs" lead="A sandbox-mode run needs an OS backend. skep probes it before every launch and refuses to start the worker when none can be applied.">
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items: start">
        <Card title="Linux: bubblewrap" icon="box">
          <p>Install the <code>bubblewrap</code> package. User namespaces must be allowed by the host, or the probe fails.</p>
          <p>
            Landlock is applied inside the sandbox when the kernel has it: abstract unix sockets
            from ABI 6 (Linux 6.12), path sockets from ABI 9. On an older kernel that step is
            skipped and <code>skep doctor</code> says so.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="macOS: Seatbelt" icon="box">
          <p>Nothing to install: <code>sandbox-exec</code> ships with macOS.</p>
          <p>It is usable when the host process is allowed to apply sandbox profiles.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Linux, opt-in: podman" icon="container">
          <p>Chosen with <code>skep onboard --sandbox podman</code>. It is never picked automatically.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Windows and others: none" icon="ban" tone="yellow">
          <p>There is no backend, and skep is not built or tested for these platforms.</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items: start">
        <CodeBlock code={bwrap} title="Linux: install bubblewrap" />
        <Callout kind="limit" title="No sandbox is an answer skep accepts">
          <p>
            <code>skep onboard --sandbox none</code> works on a host with no usable backend. Nothing
            then confines the worker process, so the policy baseline is locked with every write
            turned into a question you answer.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>
      <Terminal code={doctor} title="skep doctor, before onboarding" captured="skep doctor on a fresh throwaway SKEP_HOME, Fedora, 2026-11-18; paths shortened" />
      <p class="prose-width t-small c-mut">
        <code>blocked</code> is expected here: no profile exists until <code>skep onboard</code> creates one.
        The engine lines show which agent binaries were found and whether their sign-in variables are set.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="container" band eyebrow="Container" title="The container image" lead="The repository carries a Dockerfile and a compose file. This is what they do, read from the files themselves.">
    <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items: start">
      <div class="grid">
        <CodeBlock code={compose} title="docker compose" />
        <div class="prose">
          <ul>
            <li>The image is <code>python:3.12-slim</code> with git, bubblewrap and the GitHub CLI, and it runs <code>skep serve</code> on port 8765.</li>
            <li>Everything skep writes lives under <code>/data</code>, a named volume (<code>skep-data</code>). The container itself is disposable.</li>
            <li>The compose file passes <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> and <code>GH_TOKEN</code> through from your shell when they are set.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <Callout kind="limit" title="Not verified for this page">
          <p>
            Two things were not checked by running them: that the image named in the compose file is
            published in the registry (the file also has a build section for the checkout), and that
            sandbox mode works inside the container. The compose file adds no extra capabilities,
            and Claude Code, Codex, Aider and pi run only in sandbox mode. Read the sandbox line of
            <code>docker compose exec skep skep doctor</code> before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </Callout>
        <Callout kind="risk" title="The port is published on every interface">
          <p>
            The compose file maps <code>8765:8765</code>, so the API is reachable from your network
            and the access token is the only gate. Change the mapping to
            <code>127.0.0.1:8765:8765</code> unless you mean to share it.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="upgrade" eyebrow="Upgrade" title="Upgrading" lead="Upgrade with the tool you installed with, then restart the daemon.">
    <div class="grid">
      <Table caption="Upgrade commands">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Installed with</th><th scope="col">Upgrade</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">pipx</th><td><code>pipx upgrade skep</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">uv</th><td><code>uv tool upgrade skep</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">pip</th><td><code>pip install --upgrade skep</code>, in the same environment</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Source</th><td><code>git pull</code>, then <code>uv sync --frozen</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </Table>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>A running <code>skep serve</code> keeps the old code until you restart it.</li>
          <li>Your home (<code>~/.skep</code>) is kept. A home from an earlier release keeps deciding as it did.</li>
          <li>A home with no approved policy baseline asks before every governed action until you approve one. <code>skep onboard --check</code> shows where a home stands.</li>
          <li>What changed in each release is in the <a href="/changelog.html">changelog</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="next" band eyebrow="Next" title="The first command" lead="skep onboard connects a model, registers a work directory, records the sandbox, locks a policy baseline and opens the chat.">
    <div class="grid">
      <CodeBlock code="$ skep onboard" title="guided first run" />
      <div class="row">
        <Button href="/first-run.html" icon="arrow-right">First run, step by step</Button>
        <Button href="/cli.html" variant="ghost">CLI reference</Button>
        <Button href={PYPI_URL} variant="ghost">skep on PyPI</Button>
      </div>
      <p class="prose-width t-small c-mut">
        Something broken? <a class="c-green" href={ISSUES_URL}>Open an issue</a> and paste your
        <code>skep doctor</code> output. It prints variable names and paths, never a key.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
